Z||000000||Topknot||05-21-2001||11:28 AM||||Hallo all,<P>Ok today I have committed myself to trying the clutch removal from outta the left driver side wheel well.<P>Being I haven't done a full clutch removal and replacement this is a bit of a challenge.  Sooo...if anyone's got any hint tips or tricks do pass them on.<P>I have printed out several listings of steps to accomplishing this and have them sitting right outside with me and trying to follow them.<P>Topknot =]<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||KRMFiero||05-21-2001||12:24 PM||||Good luck, i dont see how you can do it but if you to make sure you take pictures and post how you did it and stuff||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||05-21-2001||01:27 PM||||KRM: I assume he means he's tilting the cradle and pulling the tranny. <P>If so, piece of cake. Ed Parks has a good write-up on his site. Couple of things to take note of: avoid using ATF in your clutch hydraulics like the plague, might as well replace tranny fluid while your in there(you have to drain it anyway), don't grease the tranny input shaft(light coating at most). If you don't run into any rust problems or broken bolts, it can easily be done in an afternoon. I would also stay away from the RAM clutch sets if your putting one in a 2.8. They seem to wear nicely, and engage smoothly when up to temp(mine has 25K miles and still going), but they do not clamp/connect strongly enough for the 2.8L. My RAM clutch set doensn't clamp hard enough to break the tires loose, ever. Everything else is easy piecey...||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||05-21-2001||01:34 PM||||It's easier than it seems. I bought a Luk clutch pack from Pep Boys for $120 to go in my 87 GT and I have to have &gt;3000 rpm to spin tires (new Sumitomo HTR100's) and it will chirp a nice 2nd. The Luk kit came with an alignment tool which was a plus.||||reg||14
Z||000004||NASAReeves||05-21-2001||03:22 PM||||Yes, keep us informed & take notes for us.  I'll be trying this, too, in about two months or so.<P>Good Luck!<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||14
Z||000005||Topknot||05-21-2001||03:37 PM||||Oh man,<P>Ok things are getting off to a slow start.  Here I go and dive into the job realizing that I am gonna need additional "equipment"<P>Sooo...<BR>1 - head to local Hardware store and purchase loonngg 2x4 plus 6 foot of heavy chain and links for attaching.<BR>2 - Go to take off the Driver Rear Wheel and come to find out the previous owner had one lug on each wheel with the Locking Nuts..!!! Grrr...<P>Back to Local Auto store and Tire store for solution!  <BR>1 - Purchased one 13/16 and or 21 MM 1/2 sockets with the instructions from Tire store to "hammer" them over the lock nut then work it off...so now going back to car to continue this adventure!<P>Will be having pics posted as I go along lol<P>Topknot =]<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Topknot (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
